Aerie-Purpose: Add an opt-in pref, "Restrict fonts" (aerie.fonts.restrict, off by default), that lets a page match only the CSS generic families and an allowlist of common fonts, so the fonts installed on the machine are no fingerprint surface (issue #117).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/ (css_font_selector.cc, offscreen_font_selector.cc, css_font_selector_base.cc, local_font_face_source.cc, font_restriction.h), third_party/blink/public/common/helium_noise/ (HeliumNoiseFeature::kFonts), content/browser/helium_noise/noise_token_data.cc, content/public/browser/content_browser_client.cc, chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc, components/permissions/contexts/local_fonts_permission_context.cc, chrome/browser/extensions/api/settings_private/prefs_util.cc
Aerie-Kind: hook

HeliumNoiseFeature::kFonts is a new surface token. The browser puts it in the per-site tokens
(HeliumNoiseTokenData::GetTokens) only when the profile's aerie.fonts.restrict pref is on, read
through the new ContentBrowserClient::HeliumFontRestrictionEnabled (false by default; Chrome reads
the pref). GetTokens runs only while fingerprinting protection is on for the browser and for the
site, so "token present" means "restricted here": the pref off, protection off for the whole
browser, or protection off for the site gives the real fonts, with no renderer change. Windows,
dedicated workers, shared and service workers and popups already receive these tokens (hardening/0013,
0014, 0017). The kFonts value is a derived per-site token like the others and is never read as a
number: only its presence matters. The new feature kHeliumNoiseFonts (on by default) lets the Bench
turn the surface off for its negative control.

A restricted context cannot find an installed font family that is not in the allowlist: the CSS
generic keywords, the approved list and the Microsoft aliases that fontconfig maps to Liberation
(components/aerie_fonts, one data file with unit tests). CSSFontSelector::GetFontData and
OffscreenFontSelector::GetFontData (workers' OffscreenCanvas) return no font for such a family, after
they looked for an @font-face of that name, so web fonts the page loads are never hidden. A generic
family is never filtered: the settings map it to a family of the system. FontFallbackList asks the selector (FontSelector::IsInstalledFamilyHidden) before it falls back to the system fonts for a family the selector had no font for, because that fallback would find the hidden font again. IsPlatformFamilyMatchAvailable
agrees. @font-face local() (LocalFontFaceSource) finds a font only when its full name or PostScript
name is an allowed family, or one followed by style words (for example "DejaVu Sans Bold"). The
code does not touch system fallback for missing glyphs (FontFallbackList), so text in any script
still renders, and it does not filter in FontCache itself, which has no document to ask.

Local Font Access (queryLocalFonts) is blocked by default while the pref is on:
LocalFontsPermissionContext turns "no saved choice" (ask) into block. A choice the user saved for a
site still applies, so the permission stays grantable per site.

The pref is a profile pref registered in ChromeContentBrowserClient::RegisterProfilePrefs. The
settings_private allowlist line lets the hardening page's toggle write it; the name is a string
literal there, the same as aerie_fonts::kRestrictFonts, and a test compares the two.

